 :adr/body "Six more of the twelve `:per-repository-gate` verifications were measured for
`com-aave` on 2026-09-10, all under the same PATH trap: `java`, `javac`,
`clojure` and `clj` replaced by stubs that append to a trace and exit 97,
`JAVA_HOME` unset. **The trap was proven able to fire before any empty trace
was believed**, and it fired twice during this session on paths nobody expected
-- which is the reason to keep proving it.

## Correction to ADR-2609103000

That ADR said `artifact-equivalence` has no incumbent to be equivalent to,
reading it as a claim about the shipped `built-rust-raw` artifact. **That is
wrong.** The contract defines it as

    :artifact-equivalence {:same-payload-cid true :same-definition-cids true
                           :same-exports true :same-imports true
                           :same-effects true :same-resource-bounds true
                           :wrapper-provenance-may-differ true}

which is about **the two build paths agreeing with each other** -- `amu` and
the public `kotoba` CLI -- which is also why `:single-build-path-only` is on
the `:forbidden` list. It has nothing to do with the Rust artifact. Measured
properly below, it passes.

## Where the gate stands

     1 kotoba-cli-check        PASS   new
     2 kotoba-cli-build        PASS   new
     3 amu-check               PASS   ADR-2609102000
     4 amu-compile             PASS   ADR-2609102000
     5 jvm-free-process-proof  PASS   ADR-2609102000, re-confirmed throughout
     6 artifact-equivalence    PASS   new
     7 whole-public-surface    33/34  new, one declared omission
     8 oracle-parity           PASS   ADR-2609102000 (89 agree, 2 differ)
     9 repository-tests        PASS   new, after a fix
    10 consumer-resolution     not done
    11 capability-denial       PASS   new
    12 reproducibility         PASS   new

**Ten pass, one is a stated exception, one remains.**

## artifact-equivalence: the two paths are byte-identical

Same source, same policy, same target, both JVM-free:

    amu compile          22,078 bytes  sha256 a0541c5c4cc627fb...
    kotoba -M compile    22,078 bytes  sha256 a0541c5c4cc627fb...
    provenance record    IDENTICAL
    publication record   differs in :name and its own sha256 only

The publication record names the output file, so it differs because the two
runs were told to write different filenames. That is exactly the permitted
`:wrapper-provenance-may-differ`. Every one of the six required fields agrees.

## reproducibility, with a control that failed

Two `amu compile` runs to different output paths: byte-identical, and both
reported `:definitions-recompiled 174`, so the second did the work rather than
returning a cached result.

**Then the negative control**: changing `max-limit` from 100 to 101 -- one
character -- produced a different hash. Without that, byte-identity would only
show the compiler is deterministic about *something*; with it, the hash is
known to track the input.

## capability-denial: it denies per effect, not merely on an empty set

The component requires two capabilities, `clock/now` (7) and `entropy/draw`
(23). Four policies:

    :allow #{7 23}    ok true    exit 0
    :allow #{7}       DENIED     exit 65   :kotoba/admission-denied
    :allow #{23}      DENIED     exit 65   :kotoba/admission-denied
    :allow #{}        DENIED     exit 65   :kotoba/admission-denied

The two single-capability policies are the boundary cases, and they are the
reason this counts. A check that only refuses an empty allow-set would pass
the first and last rows and tell you nothing. The refusal names its own reason
(`:error :admission`, message `capability policy denies required effects`),
so it is not being scored on an incidental failure.

**One gap in the diagnostic**: it does not name *which* effect was denied.
With two capabilities that is a short search; it will not stay short.

## whole-public-surface: 33 of 34, and the 34th is declared

The Kotoba component exports 72 names against 34 public vars in
`src/aave/main.cljc`. Ten cljc names have no identical export, and nine are
renames or decompositions -- `start!`/`start`, `persist!`/`store-put`,
`query`/`store-query`, `retract!`/`store-retract`, `fresh-store`/`store-empty`,
`entity-specs`/`entities`, `has-more-kernel?`/`has-more?`, and `paginate` and
`routes` each split into four.

The tenth is real: **`as-float` has no counterpart**, and Kotoba's
`coerce-field` handles `\"int\"` and `\"bool\"` and falls through to `:else raw`
where the cljc coerces to a double.

This is not a silent gap. The component's own header already records it, at
lines 50-53 and 679-684: `:f64` and `decimal-f64-parse` pass `amu check`, and
no entity spec in this component coerces to `:float`. Confirmed independently
-- aave's schema has 38 integer, 30 string, 4 boolean and **zero float**
fields, so the divergent branch is unreachable here.

So the language can express it and the component chose not to, with the reason
written down where the next reader will find it. Recorded as **33/34 with a
declared omission**, not as a pass and not as a defect. Scoring it green would
be exactly the reduction the contract forbids.

## repository-tests needed a fix, and the fix has a boundary

`nbb run-tests.cljs` died with `Could not find namespace: kotoba.lang.text`
while `clojure -M:test` was green. The coordinate was in `deps.edn` only, and
**nbb never reads deps.edn** (ADR-2609093000). The JVM suite reads `deps.edn`
and never loads the `.cljs`, so **the green suite was structurally incapable
of seeing the broken one.**

Fixed by copying the coordinate and its pinned sha into `nbb.edn`
(com-aave e08071a, west pin advanced ce72cba -> e08071a). Then:

    cold .nbb cache, no JVM    FAILS    nbb -> bb -> java make-classpath2
    warm .nbb cache, no JVM    PASSES   6 tests, 98 assertions, 0 failures

That is ADR-2609093000's boundary reproduced. `.nbb/` in com-aave is untracked
and not ignored; whether to commit a hash-named cache directory is left open
here rather than decided, since the hash moves with every dependency change.

## Two defects in the public CLI, found by the trap

**The public `kotoba` CLI starts a JVM.** Three subcommands shell out to
`clojure -M:run`, caught in the trace:

    clojure -M:run check <file> --source-path src
    clojure -M:run inspect <file>
    clojure -M:run definition-cids <file>

The contract says `:public-cli {... :jvm-launcher-forbidden true}` and `:amu
{:jvm-fallback :fail-closed}`. `amu --jvm-free` fails closed as specified; the
`kotoba` driver has no such flag on these paths and falls back silently. Under
a normal PATH it would have started a JVM and probably succeeded, and the
JVM-free claim for the public-cli half would have been false while looking true.

**Relative paths fail as if the file were missing.** `kotoba -M check` on a
path relative to the working directory returns `:error :decode`,
`:code :kotoba/invalid-data`, `input could not be read`. The same file by
absolute path returns `:ok true`. A genuinely nonexistent file returns **the
identical message**, so `not found` and `found but unreadable` are
indistinguishable in the output. This cost a wrong reading during this session:
the first measurement recorded `kotoba-cli-check` as failing on all three
sources, which would have made `artifact-equivalence` unreachable and the whole
contract blocked. It was a path bug.

Also noted: the contract documents `kotoba check --safe <entry>`, but the CLI
requires the `-M` execution boundary and has no `--safe`. The documented
invocation does not run.

## What is still not established

- **`consumer-resolution` remains, and `:consumer-cutover` is still false.**
  ADR-2609103000 removed the reason it looked blocked -- there is no working
  incumbent artifact and no external consumer -- but removing an obstacle is
  not the same as doing the work.
- **Only `wasm32-browser` was exercised.** The contract says
  `:required-for-every-declared-target true`.
- `src/aave/main.cljc` is still present and still the source of truth.
- The `repository-tests` pass depends on a warm `.nbb` cache. On a cold cache
  without `bb` it fails, so the JVM-free property of item 9 is conditional and
  should be read that way.

## Reproduce

Put the four refusing stubs on the front of PATH and prove one fires before
trusting an empty trace. Use `amu` at its west pin, in a detached checkout of
that pin, with `node_modules` available -- `--jvm-free` fails closed with exit
70 rather than falling back when `node_modules/nbb` is absent, which is the
contract behaving correctly.

Then, with four policy files (both capabilities, each one alone, neither):

    <pin>/bin/kotoba -M compile $PWD/src/aave/whole_component.kotoba \\
      --target wasm32-browser --policy allow.edn --output kotoba-cli.wasm
    node <pin>/bin/amu compile src/aave/whole_component.kotoba \\
      --target wasm32-browser --policy allow.edn --jvm-free --output amu.wasm
    cmp amu.wasm kotoba-cli.wasm && diff *.provenance.edn

**Note the absolute path on the `kotoba` line.** A relative one reports the
file as unreadable. Reproducibility needs its control: perturb one numeric
literal and require the hash to move."}]
